Possible sources of poly (that I can think of) are: trap and eating
chameleons. Neither are especially rare; you'll usually see several by
minesend/soko-ish. However, the odds of turning into something useful
are somewhat long.

(Monsters do not, AFAIK, zap /oPoly or fling !oPoly, for which we're
thankful in the general case but regretful here.)

You cannot pick up or wear rings nor amulets, but you can eat (some
of) them (provided you're in a form which finds them appetizing), and
get intrinsics thereby some of the time. Eating an amulet of
unchanging is possible, but just returns you to your "natural" form,
which isn't of use here.

That leaves rings. It appears to be possible to get both intrinsic
poly and intrinsic poly control by eating rings. Of the 28 possible
ring descriptions (randomized across ring types per-game), 13 are
edible by something. (12 of those, by general-purpose metallivores:
rust monsters and rock moles.)

So, you have to randomly-poly into a xorn, rock-mole, rust monster or
(longshot) gelatinous cube, get to where a ring is, hope it's a type
you can eat, hope it conveys a useful intrinsic, and hope you actually
_get_ the intrinsic in question (1-in-3 chance per ring eaten).

Given that I actually have managed to get poly'ed into a rock mole and
been two dungeon levels from an large shop containing two rings of an
edible type which price-IDs as poly control, I'd argue for "really
unlikely" rather than "practically impossible". (Of course, I died
before I got any closer than two levels away. This is "new math"
nethacking, where the actual result doesn't matter; the idea is the
important thing.)

Would tar and feathers be forthcoming were I to suggest that
bones-stuffing (using a character under no conduct restrictions) could
be considered a legitimate technique in the context of attempting an
inventoryless ascension?

(The more I fool with this, the more I'm convinced that semi-permanent
xornitude would be of enormous utility. The moreso, when it's a
sacfest- enhanced xorn with lots of extra HP and AC, and crowning.)

